Solving the Inverse Problem in the Fourier Domain
Let
\[
\widehat{f}(q_x,q_y)=\mathcal{F}\{f(x,y)\},
\]
where \(q_x\) and \(q_y\) are angular spatial frequencies in \(\mathrm{rad/m}\). The Fourier derivative properties are
\[
\mathcal{F}
\left\{
\frac{\partial^2f}{\partial x^2}
\right\}
=-q_x^2\widehat{f},
\qquad
\mathcal{F}
\left\{
\frac{\partial^2f}{\partial y^2}
\right\}
=-q_y^2\widehat{f}.
\]
Therefore,
\[
\mathcal{F}\{\nabla_\perp^2f\}
=
-\left(q_x^2+q_y^2\right)\widehat{f}.
\]
Taking the Fourier transform of the normalized forward model gives
\[
\widehat{g}
=
\left[
1+\alpha\left(q_x^2+q_y^2\right)
\right]\widehat{f}.
\]
Solving for \(\widehat{f}\),
\[
\boxed{
\widehat{f}
=
\frac{\widehat{g}}
{1+\alpha(q_x^2+q_y^2)}
}.
\]
The inverse transform gives
\[
\boxed{
f(x,y)
=
\mathcal{F}^{-1}
\left[
\frac{\mathcal{F}\{g(x,y)\}}
{1+\alpha(q_x^2+q_y^2)}
\right]
}.
\]
Substituting the definition of \(\alpha\) produces the single-distance PAD phase-retrieval formula:
\[
\boxed{
f(x,y)
=
\mathcal{F}^{-1}
\left[
\frac{\mathcal{F}\{g(x,y)\}}
{
1+
\displaystyle
\frac{\lambda z}{4\pi}
\frac{\delta}{\beta}
(q_x^2+q_y^2)
}
\right]
}.
\]

Mathematical Meaning of PAD in the Processing Workflow
The essential PAD assumption is
\[
\boxed{
\frac{\delta}{\beta}\approx\text{constant}
}.
\]
It produces the phase–attenuation relation
\[
\boxed{
\phi=-\frac{\delta}{2\beta}p
}.
\]
Combining this relation with the TIE gives
\[
\boxed{
g=\left(1-\alpha\nabla_\perp^2\right)f
},
\qquad
\boxed{
\alpha
=
\frac{\lambda z}{4\pi}
\frac{\delta}{\beta}
}.
\]
Fourier-domain inversion gives
\[
\boxed{
f
=
\mathcal{F}^{-1}
\left[
\frac{\mathcal{F}\{g\}}
{1+\alpha(q_x^2+q_y^2)}
\right]
},
\qquad
\boxed{p=-\ln f}.
\]
The derivation can be summarized as
\[
\boxed{
n=1-\delta+i\beta
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\phi=-\frac{\delta}{2\beta}p
\;\xRightarrow{\mathrm{TIE}}\;
g=(1-\alpha\nabla_\perp^2)f
\;\xRightarrow{\mathcal{F}^{-1}}\;
f,\;p
}.
\]
This is the mathematical basis for converting a propagation-based phase-contrast projection into an attenuation projection suitable for conventional CT reconstruction.
